# Short introduction to Rudof

```elixir
Mix.install(rudof_elixir: [path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)], kino: "== 0.19.0")
```

## Introduction

This Livebook contains a teaser introduction to [rudof](https://rudof-project.github.io/) via the Elixir bindings in `rudof_elixir`. Its setup installs the wrapper and Kino for Livebook displays.

Back to the [__Table of Contents__](intro.livemd).

## Install and initialize rudof

The main entry point is the `Rudof.Session` module through which most of the functionality is provided. In order to initialize that session, it is possible to pass upstream settings as an Elixir keyword list through the `config` option to `Session.new!/1`; this example sets a base IRI. Rust parses and validates the settings using its TOML configuration API. Alternatively, use `path: file_path` for a TOML file or `toml_str: toml_string` for TOML text. With no options, `Session.new!()` uses defaults. `Session` wraps one native state and delegates work directly to Rust.

```elixir
alias Rudof.Session
session = Session.new!(config: [base_iri: "https://example.net/"])
```

Rudof applies that base when parsing relative IRIs. Serialize the data to see how the configured values expand:

```elixir
:ok = Session.read_data(session, "<alice> <knows> <bob> .")
{:ok, configured_data} = Session.serialize_data(session, :ntriples)
configured_data
```

The `Rudof.Session` module contains the methods that provide the main functionality. For example, `Session.version/1` retrieves the current version of the library.

```elixir
{:ok, version} = Session.version(session)
```

## Working with the Elixir Wrapper

This is a compact tour of the wrapper's most useful patterns. For the complete
explanation of configuration, inputs, errors and session ownership, open
[Getting Started](../getting_started.livemd). The chapters in this tutorial
teach RDF, SPARQL and validation; the wrapper notebook focuses on using them
from Elixir.

### Choose how to manage state

| Pattern | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `Rudof.query/3`, `convert/4`, validation helpers | One independent operation with automatic cleanup |
| `Session.with_session/2` | A complete workflow that shares state and closes automatically |
| `Session.new/1` or `new!/1`, then `close/1` | Interactive work across cells or a longer-lived application process |

Our example is a project's maintainer directory. Alice has an email address;
Bob has not supplied one. Namespace declarations are collected here so the
examples below can use compact names.

```elixir
alias Rudof.{Error, Visualization}

namespace = "http://example.org/"
turtle_prefixes = "@prefix : <#{namespace}> .\n"
sparql_prefixes = "PREFIX : <#{namespace}>\n"
shex_prefixes = "PREFIX : <#{namespace}>\nPREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>\n"

data = turtle_prefixes <> """
:rudof_elixir :maintainer :alice, :bob .
:alice :name "Alice" ; :email "alice@example.org" .
:bob :name "Bob" .
"""

contacts_query = sparql_prefixes <> """
SELECT ?name ?email WHERE {
  :rudof_elixir :maintainer ?person .
  ?person :name ?name .
  OPTIONAL { ?person :email ?email }
}
ORDER BY ?name
"""
```

### Query once, or combine several operations

`Rudof.query/3` creates a fresh session and closes it before returning. SELECT
results have aligned variable names and rows of **RDF term strings**; quotes,
datatypes and language tags are retained. Bob's unbound email is `nil`.
`Visualization.table_data/1` adapts those rows for Kino without changing values.

```elixir
{:ok, contacts} = Rudof.query(data, contacts_query)
Kino.DataTable.new(Visualization.table_data(contacts))
```

For a job that also needs an export, `with_session` lets both operations reuse
the loaded graph. The callback's value is returned directly: here it is a map,
without another `{:ok, ...}` around it. The Elixir `with` returns the first error
if a step fails. The session closes after the callback returns, raises, throws
or exits; exceptions still propagate. Keep all session work inside the callback.

```elixir
bundle =
  Session.with_session(fn job ->
    with :ok <- Session.read_data(job, data),
         {:ok, contacts} <- Session.query(job, contacts_query),
         {:ok, ntriples} <- Session.serialize_data(job, :ntriples) do
      %{contacts: contacts, ntriples: ntriples}
    end
  end)

Kino.Text.new(bundle.ntriples)
```

### Reuse a session, validate, and repair

Continue with the session created above. Reset its working state, register the
default namespace for compact selectors, and load the maintainer directory.
The session retains its configuration.

```elixir
:ok = Session.reset(session)
:ok = Session.add_prefix(session, "", namespace)
:ok = Session.read_data(session, data)

schema = shex_prefixes <> """
:Maintainer { :name xsd:string ; :email xsd:string }
"""

:ok = Session.read_shex(session, schema)
:ok = Session.read_shapemap(session, ":bob@:Maintainer")
{:ok, before_repair} = Session.validate_shex(session)
false = before_repair.conforms
Kino.DataTable.new(before_repair.entries)
```

Nonconforming data is a successful validation result: inspect `conforms` and
`entries`. Parsing and execution failures instead return `{:error, %Error{}}`.
Reads merge by default, so adding Bob's email keeps the existing graph and
loaded schema. Pass `merge: false` when you want to replace the data.

```elixir
:ok = Session.read_data(session, turtle_prefixes <> ~s(:bob :email "bob@example.org" .))
{:ok, after_repair} = Session.validate_shex(session)
true = after_repair.conforms
{:ok, updated_contacts} = Session.query(session, contacts_query)
Kino.DataTable.new(Visualization.table_data(updated_contacts))
```

The earlier report remains an ordinary Elixir value describing the earlier
state. Query and validate again to obtain updated results. A session handle
shares mutable state; assigning it to another variable does not clone its graph.

### Display native output

Rudof generates the diagram through its native PlantUML serializer.
`Visualization` translates the supported output to Mermaid for Livebook.
This display needs no external image renderer.

```elixir
{:ok, plantuml} = Session.serialize_data(session, :plantuml)
{:ok, diagram} = Rudof.Visualization.plantuml_to_mermaid(plantuml)
Kino.Mermaid.new(diagram)
```

### Know the input and lifetime rules

Reads such as `read_data/3` accept source text; for files or URLs, use explicit
inputs with `load_data/3`, such as `[{:file, path}]` or `[{:url, url}]`. Formats
are atoms: session loading uses `format:`, while one-off query and validation
helpers use `data_format:`. Configuration uses upstream names, with one of
`config: keywords`, `toml_str: text`, or `path: filename`; `with_session` takes
these options as its second argument.

Unknown options and duplicate keyword keys are rejected instead of ignored:

```elixir
{:error, %Error{kind: :invalid_argument}} =
  Session.read_data(session, data, formatt: :turtle)
```

A session closes when its creator process exits. Create a session inside each
Task for independent jobs, or use the one-off helpers there. Calls on one
session run sequentially; several calls do not form a transaction. Calls wait
without a timeout, and timing out a caller does not cancel native work.

Close an interactive session when finished. If you reevaluate its creation
cell, close the previous handle first. The full
[Getting Started notebook](../getting_started.livemd) explains these rules and
shows configuration files, exports and concurrent jobs.

## Close the session

Release the native state when finished. To revisit earlier cells, evaluate from the beginning.

```elixir
:ok = Session.close(session)
```
